{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_qeupoj45dglsonh4km6qsp6qdu6hox7snr6ydtrysogbz4tworqq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. The library turned out to be small (one source file), so I translated it directly rather than delegating.\n\n## Surface enumeration (definition of done)\nBuilt the C library with CMake and ran `nm -D libStaticAlias.so`:\n\n| C export | Rust export |\n|---|---|\n| `T static_alias` | ✅ `T static_alias` |\n| `T driver` | ✅ `T driver` |\n\n`diff` of the two `T`-symbol lists is **empty** — no namespace/renaming macros exist in `include/staticalias.h`, so the linker names equal the source names.\n\n## Translation (`translation/src/lib.rs`)\n- **`crate-type = [\"cdylib\"]`**, `[lib] name = \"StaticAlias\"` so the artifact is `libStaticAlias.so`, matching CMake.\n- `#[unsafe(no_mangle)] pub unsafe extern \"C\"` with exact signatures (`*mut c_int -> *mut c_int`, `(c_int, c_int) -> ()`).\n- The function-local `static int inner = 1;` became a `static mut INNER: c_int = 1;` in the library's writable data, so it persists across calls and its address can be returned to callers (the whole point of this library).\n- Faithfully preserved the aliasing subtleties:\n  - `*outer` is re-read after the write to `inner`, since `outer` may alias `inner`.\n  - In `driver`, `initial_value` is copied into a local slot first, because `&initial_value` is the address of the *callee's own parameter copy*, which `static_alias` may mutate through the alias.\n  - Comparison/branch order (`*outer >= inner`) and which pointer is returned in each arm are unchanged.\n- Signed overflow (`inner += *outer`, `*outer += inner`) is UB in C but wraps in practice; used `wrapping_add` to reproduce the observed two's-complement behavior rather than panicking. No bugs were \"fixed\".\n- Output goes through **libc `printf`** via an `extern \"C\"` declaration, not Rust's `println!`. This matters: Rust's stdout is a separate buffer that would never be flushed when the `.so` is loaded by a C driver, and interleaving/flush timing must match byte-for-byte.\n\n## Verification beyond compilation\nBuilt a `dlopen`-based C harness and byte-compared stdout of the C `.so` vs the Rust `.so`:\n- Hand-picked cases: zero/negative/oversized iteration counts, `INT_MAX`/`INT_MIN` initial values, repeated `driver` calls sharing accumulated static state, direct `static_alias` calls checking *which* pointer was returned (`ret == &local`), and mixed sequences feeding the returned pointer back in.\n- 300 randomized cases across all three modes, compared with `cmp` (strict byte equality).\n\nAll passed with zero mismatches; `cargo build --release` is warning-free."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":199,"cache_read_input_tokens":24840,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":199,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2c1b53f4-6ac2-484a-9b7f-b6596bd9b08a","uuid":"2651ff12-93f7-4e26-9e1a-5e28ec62fccd","timestamp":"2026-08-28T10:01:04.342Z"}
